"Should it work if I mix the memory by adding two Kingston HyperX 2666 modules?"

Google AI:

Yes, it will work, but it will slow down your computer's performance.
While the system will boot and recognize the total capacity (32GB), mixing RAM kits forces your computer to make compromises to maintain stability.

The Cost of Mixing RAM

• Speed Drops to the Lowest Common Denominator: Your system cannot run different sticks at different speeds. Adding the 2666MHz Kingston modules will force your fast 3600MHz Corsair RAM to downclock and run at 2666MHz.

• Ryzen Performance Hit: AMD Ryzen 5000 processors are highly sensitive to RAM speeds. Dropping your memory speed from 3600MHz to 2666MHz will cause a noticeable drop in gaming frame rates (FPS) and general system responsiveness, as the CPU's internal clock (Infinity Fabric) slows down to match the RAM.

• Potential Instability: Even if you accept the lower speed, mixing different brands, memory chips, and timings can occasionally cause random blue screens (BSODs) or boot failures.

🛠️ How to Do It Correctly (If You Proceed)

If you absolutely need the extra capacity for heavy multitasking or production work and don't mind the gaming performance loss, install them like this:

1. Alternate the Slots: Put the Corsair sticks in slots 2 and 4, and the Kingston sticks in slots 1 and 3 (counting away from the CPU).

2. Clear BIOS settings: Before installing the new sticks, turn off your PC and clear any existing XMP profiles in your BIOS so the system boots at standard default speeds first.

Recommendation

If this is primarily a gaming PC, stick with the 16GB of Corsair 3600MHz RAM...
